phytes). This specimen dataset includes over 4.7 million records (4.3 million vascular plants and 380,000 bryophytes).","url":"","emllink":"http://ipt.mobot.org:8080/ipt/eml.do?r=tropicosspecimens","archivelink":"http://ipt.mobot.org:8080/ipt/archive.do?r=tropicosspecimens","contacts":[{"first_name":"Jordan","last_name":"Teisher","role":"Curator and Director of the Herbarium","email":"jteisher@mobot.org"},{"first_name":"Heather","last_name":"Stimmel","role":"Programmer Analyst","email":"heather.stimmel@mobot.org"},{"first_name":"Heather","last_name":"Stimmel","email":"heather.stimmel@mobot.org"},{"first_name":"Jordan","last_name":"Teisher","role":"Curator and Director of the Herbarium","email":"jteisher@mobot.org"}],"data_rights":"CC4 BY","publisher":"eaa701aa-2b56-4058-bbe7-c0bea9c1661d","totalCount":4496509,"hitRatio":2.2239475112804179e-7},{"uuid":"6258d160-a7aa-4937-bce3-3538eebd374f","itemCount":1,"name":"Duke University Vascular Plant Collection","description":"Duke Herbarium houses more than 425,000 specimens of vascular plants, including 821 types. The collection is especially rich in accessions from the Southeastern United States, in particular for the Carolinas, and Mesoamerica. The vascular plant herbarium contains an important collection of over 22,000 sheets from La Selva&nbsp;in Costa Rica.","logo":"https://sernecportal.org/portal/content/collicon/duke.png","url":null,"emllink":"https://sernecportal.org/portal/collections/datasets/emlhandler.php?collid=192","archivelink":"https://sernecportal.org/portal/content/dwca/duke_dwc-a.zip","contacts":[{"email":"hbrown@mailbox.sc.edu"},{"email":"hbrown@mailbox.sc.edu"},{"email":"mdw26@duke.edu"},{"first_name":"Michael D.","last_name":"Windham","email":"mdw26@duke.edu"},{"first_name":"Michael D.","last_name":"Windham","email":"mdw26@duke.edu"}],"data_rights":"CC4 BY-NC","publisher":"42f2a0a4-5981-4874-88d5-9efb1e8d52c9","totalCount":157944,"hitRatio":0.000006331357949653042},{"uuid":"667a12e7-c6d8-4de0-933f-ce2f07cb7a92","itemCount":1,"name":"Baylor University Herbarium","description":"The Baylor University Herbarium was officially founded in 1967 as part of the Department of Biology at Baylor University (BAYLU). Currently located on the 2nd floor, A-wing, of the Baylor Sciences Building, the herbarium was organized by Dr. Floyd F. Davidson in 1967, then chair of the department, with the first curator Ms. Fannie Mae Hurst-Windham. Much of the herbarium is dedicated to Texas flora, though with specimens included from Oklahoma, Louisiana, Central/South America, and China. The herbarium also houses over 200+ specimens from Lindheimer, specifically those specimens near New Braunfels and Comanche Spring. Dr. Walter Holmes, Professor Emeritus of Biology at Baylor University, is the most recent curator of the herbarium who has specialized in the genus Mikania (Asteraceae) with the herbarium containing several types specimens.","logo":"https://portal.torcherbaria.org/portal/content/collicon/baylu-baylu.png","url":null,"emllink":"https://sernecportal.org/portal/collections/datasets/emlhandler.php?collid=463","archivelink":"https://portal.torcherbaria.org/portal/content/dwca/baylu-baylu_dwc-a.zip","contacts":[{"email":"hbrown@mailbox.sc.edu"},{"email":"hbrown@mailbox.sc.edu"},{"email":"Joseph_D_White@baylor.edu"},{"last_name":"Joseph White","email":"Joseph_D_White@baylor.edu"},{"last_name":"Joseph White","email":"Joseph_D_White@baylor.edu"}],"data_rights":"CC0","publisher":"a9684883-ce9b-4be1-9841-b063fc69e163","totalCount":61178,"hitRatio":0.000016345745202523783},{"uuid":"70abba3e-5f2a-4276-87f3-261706e24453","itemCount":1,"name":"Botanical Research Institute of Texas, Philecology Herbarium","description":"The core of BRIT&rsquo;s herbarium is the Lloyd H. Shinners Collection in Systematic Botany, begun at Southern Methodist University in Dallas, Texas, in 1944. The Botanical Research Institute of Texas was formed in 1987 to house the transfer of this collection, which moved to Fort Worth in 1991.&nbsp; Areas of geographic concentration encompass Texas, the southeastern United States, Mexico, Central America, South America, and Southeast Asia. The BRIT herbarium holdings are worldwide in scope, and represent most of the earth&rsquo;s plant families.","logo":"https://portal.torcherbaria.org/portal/content/collicon/britbrit.png","url":"https://fwbg.org/research/herbarium/about-the-brit-herbarium/","emllink":"https://sernecportal.org/portal/collections/datasets/emlhandler.php?collid=370","archivelink":"https://portal.torcherbaria.org/portal/content/dwca/brit-brit_dwc-a.zip","contacts":[{"email":"hbrown@mailbox.sc.edu"},{"email":"hbrown@mailbox.sc.edu"},{"email":"trehman@brit.org"},{"first_name":"Tiana","last_name":"Rehman","role":"Herbarium Director","email":"trehman@brit.org"},{"first_name":"Ashley","last_name":"Bales","role":"Collections Manager","email":"abales@fwbg.org"},{"first_name":"Tiana","last_name":"Rehman","role":"Herbarium Director","email":"trehman@brit.org"},{"first_name":"Ashley","last_name":"Bales","role":"Collections Manager","email":"abales@fwbg.org"}],"data_rights":"CC0","publisher":"a9684883-ce9b-4be1-9841-b063fc69e163","totalCount":772449,"hitRatio":0.0000012945838495486434},{"uuid":"79aa7602-a963-44f3-82dd-e141a387adb8","itemCount":1,"name":"Morehead State University Herbarium","description":"","url":null,"emllink":"https://sernecportal.org/portal/collections/datasets/emlhandler.php?collid=266","archivelink":"https://sernecportal.org/portal/content/dwca/mdky_dwc-a.zip","contacts":[{"email":"hbrown@mailbox.sc.edu"},{"email":"hbrown@mailbox.sc.edu"},{"email":"a.risk@moreheadstate.edu"},{"last_name":"Allen Risk","email":"a.risk@moreheadstate.edu"},{"last_name":"Allen Risk","email":"a.risk@moreheadstate.edu"}],"data_rights":"CC4 BY-NC","publisher":"42f2a0a4-5981-4874-88d5-9efb1e8d52c9","totalCount":10415,"hitRatio":0.00009601536245799327},{"uuid":"7fbf76b1-6bd6-4217-a5bc-1d89e45f6a68","itemCount":1,"name":"CSLA - Cal State LA Herbarium","description":"The CSLA Herbarium holds approximately 35,000 dry-mounted vascular plant specimens from around the world, with particular collection emphasis in Southern California and Northern Mexico. Founded in 1956, CSLA contains significant collections from J. Henrickson, B. Prigge, and R. M. Straw.","logo":"https://cch2.org/portal/content/collicon/csla.png","url":null,"emllink":"https://cch2.org/portal/content/dwca/csla_dwc-a.eml","archivelink":"https://cch2.org/portal/content/dwca/csla_dwc-a.zip","contacts":[{"email":"jyost@calpoly.edu"},{"email":"jyost@calpoly.edu"},{"email":"kfisher2@exchange.calstatela.edu"},{"first_name":"Kirsten","last_name":"Fisher","role":"Curator","email":"kfisher2@exchange.calstatela.edu"},{"first_name":"Kirsten","last_name":"Fisher","role":"Curator","email":"kfisher2@exchange.calstatela.edu"}],"data_rights":"CC4 BY-NC","publisher":"6cf8576a-769e-46ca-a6bd-3f1e1f7a3447","totalCount":26689,"hitRatio":0.00003746862003072427},{"uuid":"92e4e092-6dcb-46bc-85a0-dea8310aba45","itemCount":1,"name":"Troy Herbarium Specimens from Morphbank","description":"Specimen Information and Images in the Morphbank repository from herarium specimens housed in the Troy State University (Alabama) Herbarium (TROY). 14,534 records in total. Digitized as part of the The Deep South Plant Specimen Imaging Project. http://www.nsf.gov/awardsearch/showAward?AWD_ID=0646222","url":"http://spectrum.troy.edu/herbarium/","emllink":"http://services.morphbank.net/ipt-2.0.4/eml.do?r=troyherbarium","archivelink":"http://services.morphbank.net/ipt-2.0.4/archive.do?r=troyherbarium","contacts":[{"first_name":"Greg","last_name":"Riccardi","role":"Director","email":"griccardi@fsu.edu"},{"first_name":"Deborah","last_name":"Paul","role":"Manager","email":"dpaul@fsu.edu"}],"data_rights":"Unknown License, assume Public Domain","publisher":"1a3c16b9-e699-439d-9ded-89dbbc84a100","totalCount":14534,"hitRatio":0.0000688041832943443},{"uuid":"ddef79ec-043c-4027-9876-c4a298feff6d","itemCount":1,"name":"Florida State University, Robert K. Godfrey Herbarium","description":"The collection contains >220,000 specimens that document the distribution and natural variation of plants and algae worldwide, with a special focus on the 2,400 species of plants found in N FL--one of North America's biodiversity hotspots--and the microalgae of Florida's Gulf and Atlantic coasts.","logo":"https://sernecportal.org/portal/content/collicon/fsu.png","url":"http://herbarium.bio.fsu.edu/","emllink":"https://sernecportal.org/portal/content/dwca/fsu_dwc-a.eml","archivelink":"https://sernecportal.org/portal/content/dwca/fsu_dwc-a.zip","contacts":[{"email":"hbrown@mailbox.sc.edu"},{"email":"hbrown@mailbox.sc.edu"},{"email":"amast@bio.fsu.edu"},{"last_name":"Dr. Austin R. Mast","email":"amast@bio.fsu.edu"},{"last_name":"Dr. Austin R. Mast","email":"amast@bio.fsu.edu"}],"data_rights":"CC3 BY-NC","publisher":"42f2a0a4-5981-4874-88d5-9efb1e8d52c9","totalCount":85749,"hitRatio":0.000011661943579516963},{"uuid":"f1cf8457-237e-487a-9d13-5de7d81b9de4","itemCount":1,"name":"University of South Florida Herbarium (USF)","description":"The USF Herbarium was established as a research and teaching collection in 1958 by George R. Cooley, two years after the founding of the University of South Florida. The USF Herbarium is the second largest collection in Florida, the seventh largest in the southeastern United States, and ranks in the upper third of the world's herbaria in size. The herbarium contains approximately 280,000 specimens, consisting of about 260,000 specimens of vascular plants, 14,000 algae, 2,800 bryophytes, 1,300 lichens, and 300 macrofungi. The collection contains approximately 400 type specimens. The herbarium is richest in specimens from Florida (~40%), with additional holdings from North America north of Mexico (~35%), Latin America and the West Indies (~15%), and the Eastern Hemisphere (~10%). Specific groups well represented in the herbarium include Acanthaceae, Apocynaceae, Begoniaceae, Bromeliaceae, Fabaceae, Orchidaceae, and pteridophytes (ferns).","logo":"https://cdn.plantatlas.org/img/logos/usf-v-black.jpg","url":"http://biology.usf.edu/cmmb/research/herbarium/","emllink":"https://ipt.plantatlas.usf.edu/eml.do?r=usf_herb","archivelink":"https://ipt.plantatlas.usf.edu/archive.do?r=usf_herb","contacts":[{"first_name":"Alan R.","last_name":"Franck","role":"Curator","email":"afranck@mail.usf.edu"},{"first_name":"Alan R.","last_name":"Franck","role":"Curator","email":"afranck@mail.usf.edu"},{"first_name":"Keith","last_name":"Bornhorst","role":"IT","email":"bornhorst@usf.edu"},{"first_name":"Bruce F.","last_name":"Hansen","role":"Associated Research Staff","email":"hansen@usf.edu"},{"first_name":"Alan R.","last_name":"Franck","role":"Curator","email":"afranck@mail.usf.edu"}],"data_rights":"CC4 BY","publisher":"ce4b086a-5805-4edf-8044-4593f62f4790","totalCount":240829,"hitRatio":0.0000041523238480415564}],"aggs":{"rs":{"doc_count_error_upper_bound":0,"sum_other_doc_count":0,"buckets":[{"key":"beab5209-9628-4d4d-851e-2bc9bb1a0105","doc_count":5},{"key":"e7016bd5-cb10-45b9-8959-0f5750f7a5db","doc_count":3},{"key":"4e3043a6-d48a-4a35-b5fb-f67d50cbc158","doc_count":2},{"key":"3056112e-97c6-4d0d-b6c2-3c0a9adaca24","doc_count":1},{"key":"5386d272-06c6-4027-b5d5-d588c2afe5e5","doc_count":1},{"key":"6258d160-a7aa-4937-bce3-3538eebd374f","doc_count":1},{"key":"667a12e7-c6d8-4de0-933f-ce2f07cb7a92","doc_count":1},{"key":"70abba3e-5f2a-4276-87f3-261706e24453","doc_count":1},{"key":"79aa7602-a963-44f3-82dd-e141a387adb8","doc_count":1},{"key":"7fbf76b1-6bd6-4217-a5bc-1d89e45f6a68","doc_count":1},{"key":"92e4e092-6dcb-46bc-85a0-dea8310aba45","doc_count":1},{"key":"ddef79ec-043c-4027-9876-c4a298feff6d","doc_count":1},{"key":"f1cf8457-237e-487a-9d13-5de7d81b9de4","doc_count":1}]},"unique_scientific_names":{"doc_count_error_upper_bound":0,"sum_other_doc_count":0,"buckets":[{"key":"spergula arvensis","doc_count":20}]},"max_dm":{"value":177696